var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"bibliography/#Bibliography","page":"Bibliography","text":"Higham, N. J. (2002). Accuracy and Stability of Numerical Algorithms. Second Edition (Society for Industrial and Applied Mathematics).\n\n\n\nLegat, B.; Parrilo, P. and Jungers, R. (2020). Certifying Unstability of Switched Systems Using Sum of Squares Programming. SIAM Journal on Control and Optimization 58, 2616–2638.\n\n\n\nTrefethen, L. N. and Schreiber, R. S. (1990). Average-Case Stability of Gaussian Elimination. SIAM Journal on Matrix Analysis and Applications 11, 335–360.\n\n\n\nWedin, P.-Å. (1972). Perturbation bounds in connection with singular value decomposition. BIT Numerical Mathematics 12, 99–111.\n\n\n\n","title":"Bibliography"},{"category":"section","location":"#MultivariateMoments-—-Multivariate-Moments-for-Julia","page":"Index","text":"Extension of MultivariatePolynomials to moments of multivariate measures and their scalar product with polynomials. It also includes the extraction of atomic measures from moment matrices which is related to the moment problem.","title":"MultivariateMoments — Multivariate Moments for Julia"},{"category":"section","location":"#Contents","page":"Index","text":"Pages = [\"moments.md\", \"atoms.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/dependence_tolerance/#Tolerance-of-the-numerical-dependence-of-monomials","page":"Tolerance of the numerical dependence of monomials","text":"In the SVD examples below, atom extraction from a moment matrix and reduction modulo a truncated ideal give a matrix Z with orthonormal columns. Its rows are indexed by monomials. Which rows are linearly dependent on the previous ones?\n\nFor atom extraction, Z spans the image space of the moment matrix, see image_space. The moment matrix is typically the solution of an SDP solver hence it is only known with the accuracy of the solver.\nFor a truncated ideal, Z spans the null space of the Macaulay matrix of exact equations, see MacaulayNullspace. It is used, e.g., by SumOfSquares to reduce the Gram basis of a Sum-of-Squares certificate over an algebraic set.\n\nIn both cases, Z is computed from a singular value decomposition and the dependent monomials are the columns that are not pivots of the reduced row echelon form of Z^top. The Gaussian elimination needs a tolerance below which an entry is considered to be zero. This tutorial studies how to choose this tolerance so that it works for both cases. It explains the choice made in accuracy.\n\nusing LinearAlgebra, Markdown, Printf, Random\nimport GenericLinearAlgebra # `svd` of `Matrix{BigFloat}`\nusing DynamicPolynomials\nimport MultivariateBases as MB\nimport MultivariateMoments as MM\nimport SparseArrays","title":"Tolerance of the numerical dependence of monomials"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/dependence_tolerance/#Noise-and-signal","page":"Tolerance of the numerical dependence of monomials","text":"We compute the true pivots by doing the same computation in BigFloat. To make sure the reference is not affected by the round-off errors, the elimination in BigFloat uses a tolerance much smaller than the precision of Float64 but much larger than the precision of BigFloat.\n\nsetprecision(BigFloat, 128)\n\nfunction pivots(A, tol)\n    U = copy(A)\n    MM.RowEchelon.rref!(U, tol)\n    return Set(MM._pivots(U))\nend\n\ntrue_pivots(A::Matrix{BigFloat}) = pivots(A, big(1e-25))\ntrue_pivots (generic function with 1 method)\n\nGiven the true pivots, we run the Gaussian elimination by following them instead of comparing the entries with a tolerance. Along the way, we record:\n\nthe noise: the largest entry of a column that is truly dependent; it should be zero but it is nonzero because of numerical errors,\nthe signal: the smallest pivot of a column that is truly independent.\n\nA tolerance tol gives the true pivots if noise < tol < signal.\n\nfunction noise_and_signal(A, piv)\n    A = copy(A)\n    nr, nc = size(A)\n    i = 1\n    noise = 0.0\n    signal = Inf\n    for j in 1:nc\n        if i > nr\n            break\n        end\n        m, mi = findmax(abs, view(A, i:nr, j))\n        mi += i - 1\n        if j in piv\n            signal = min(signal, m)\n            A[[i, mi], :] = A[[mi, i], :]\n            A[i, :] ./= A[i, j]\n            for k in 1:nr\n                if k != i\n                    A[k, :] .-= A[k, j] .* A[i, :]\n                end\n            end\n            i += 1\n        else\n            noise = max(noise, m)\n            A[i:nr, j] .= 0\n        end\n    end\n    return noise, signal\nend\nnoise_and_signal (generic function with 1 method)\n\nFor each case, the first table gives the number of monomials n, the measured noise and signal, and two ratios. With r the selected rank, ρ₁ = σ[r+1] / σ[1] scales the first discarded singular value by the largest one, and ρᵣ = σ[r+1] / σ[r] scales it by the weakest retained one.\n\nThe second table shows which tolerances recover the reference pivots (✓). The first two columns of results use ρ₁/√n (previously used by Echelon) and ρᵣ·√n. Both floor the ratio at machine precision before scaling and apply a single elimination. Gap at r checks for a gap at the selected rank, and First gap uses the first gap at or after that rank. Both call BasisDependence and retry at a lower tolerance if the initial elimination loses rank. For example, a rank-one space spanned by a vector with 128 equal entries has unit norm after normalization, but each entry is only about 0.088. If the proposed tolerance is 0.09, the first pass discards every entry.\n\nfunction gap_pivots(Z, basis, σ, r; gap_tol = 1e-2)\n    return map((false, true)) do search_later\n        policy = MM.GapBasedAccuracy(MM.FixedRank(r); gap_tol, search_later)\n        null = MM.MacaulayNullspace(Z, basis, MM.accuracy(σ, r, policy))\n        d = MM.BasisDependence{MM.LinearDependence}(null)\n        return Set(findall(!MM.is_dependent, d.dependence))\n    end\nend\n\nfunction row(labels, Z, basis, σ, r, piv)\n    A = Matrix(transpose(Z))\n    n = size(Z, 1)\n    noise, signal = noise_and_signal(A, piv)\n    label = join(labels, \" | \")\n    measurements = @sprintf(\n        \"| %s | %d | %.1e | %.1e | %.1e | %.1e |\",\n        label,\n        n,\n        σ[r+1] / σ[1],\n        σ[r+1] / σ[r],\n        noise,\n        signal,\n    )\n    recovery = \"| $label |\"\n    for tol in (\n        max(σ[r+1] / σ[1], eps()) / sqrt(n),\n        max(σ[r+1] / σ[r], eps()) * sqrt(n),\n    )\n        recovery *= pivots(A, tol) == piv ? \" ✓ |\" : \" ✗ |\"\n    end\n    for found in gap_pivots(Z, basis, σ, r)\n        recovery *= found == piv ? \" ✓ |\" : \" ✗ |\"\n    end\n    return (; measurements, recovery)\nend\n\nfunction table(rows, labels = (\"Case\",))\n    header = join(labels, \" | \")\n    sep = repeat(\" --- |\", length(labels))\n    return Markdown.parse(\"\"\"\n    | $header | n | ρ₁ | ρᵣ | Noise | Signal |\n    |$sep --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n    $(join((row.measurements for row in rows), \"\\n\"))\n\n    **Recovery of the reference pivots**\n\n    | $header | ρ₁/√n | ρᵣ·√n | Gap at r | First gap |\n    |$sep --- | --- | --- | --- |\n    $(join((row.recovery for row in rows), \"\\n\"))\n    \"\"\")\nend\ntable (generic function with 2 methods)","title":"Noise and signal"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/dependence_tolerance/#Macaulay-null-spaces","page":"Tolerance of the numerical dependence of monomials","text":"The Macaulay matrix of degree D of exact equations has entries known up to machine precision so its singular values have a clear gap between the nonzero singular values and the ones that are zero up to round-off errors.\n\n@polyvar x y z\n\nfunction macaulay(label, equalities, D)\n    M = MB.macaulay(equalities, D, [x, y, z])\n    A = float(Matrix(SparseArrays.sparse(M)))\n    F = svd(A; full = true)\n    check = MM._default_nullspace_rank_check(A)\n    r = MM.rank_from_singular_values(F.S, check)\n    Z = F.Vt[(r+1):end, :]'\n    big_A = big.(A)\n    ref = svd(big_A; full = true)\n    ref_rank = MM.rank_from_singular_values(\n        ref.S,\n        MM._default_nullspace_rank_check(big_A),\n    )\n    @assert r == ref_rank\n    piv = true_pivots(Matrix(ref.Vt[(ref_rank+1):end, :]))\n    return row((label,), Z, M.column_basis, F.S, r, piv)\nend\nmacaulay (generic function with 1 method)\n\nThe four systems below are truncated at degree D.\n\nM1 (D = 5) uses x² + y² = 1 and xy = z.\nM2 (D = 5) uses x² + y²/3 = 0.7 and xy = z³/7.\nM3 (D = 6) uses x² + 0.001y² = 0.7 and xy = 1000z³.\nM4 (D = 5) uses x³ − 0.3xy + 1/7 = 0, y² = 3xz, and z² = x + 0.25.\n\ntable([\n    macaulay(\"M1\", [x^2 + y^2 - 1.0, x * y - z], 5),\n    macaulay(\"M2\", [x^2 + 1 / 3 * y^2 - 0.7, x * y - 1 / 7 * z^3], 5),\n    macaulay(\"M3\", [x^2 + 1e-3 * y^2 - 0.7, x * y - 1e3 * z^3], 6),\n    macaulay(\"M4\", [x^3 - 0.3x * y + 1 / 7, y^2 - 3x * z, z^2 - x - 0.25], 5),\n])\n  Case  n      ρ₁      ρᵣ   Noise  Signal\n  –––– –– ––––––– ––––––– ––––––– –––––––\n    M1 53 4.4e-16 1.7e-15 6.7e-16 3.1e-01\n    M2 56 4.4e-16 1.7e-15 3.3e-16 1.1e-01\n    M3 84 4.5e-16 6.3e-13 3.8e-14 4.0e-01\n    M4 56 4.4e-16 1.1e-14 4.4e-16 2.6e-01\n\n  \u001b[1mRecovery of the reference pivots\u001b[22m\n\n  Case ρ₁/√n ρᵣ·√n Gap at r First gap\n  –––– ––––– ––––– –––––––– –––––––––\n    M1     ✗     ✓        ✓         ✓\n    M2     ✗     ✓        ✓         ✓\n    M3     ✗     ✓        ✓         ✓\n    M4     ✗     ✓        ✓         ✓\n\nIn M3 the equations have very different scales. The noise in the elimination is about 1e-13 even though σ[r+1] / σ[1] is close to machine precision. Scaling by σ[r] gives a larger tolerance.\n\nWedin bounds the angle between singular subspaces using perturbation or residual norms and the spectral separation of retained and discarded directions. With a pronounced gap at rank r, that separation may be close to σ[r]. Using σ[r+1] as a proxy for the perturbation size then suggests the scale σ[r+1] / σ[r]. An actual angle bound needs independent information about the perturbation. (Wedin, 1972)\n\nGaussian elimination introduces rounding error and can amplify errors already present in the singular vectors. For partial pivoting, a worst-case backward error bound includes a dimension factor and element growth (Chapter 9). The square-root rule of thumb for accumulated rounding error assumes cancellation of errors of varying signs (Section 2.8). (Higham, 2002)\n\nAverage-case experiments show much smaller element growth than the worst-case bound for several random matrix ensembles. (Trefethen and Schreiber, 1990)\n\nApplying these results to dependence decisions requires an additional empirical choice. Here the input may already contain error from an SDP solver. The multiplier √n for the initial pivot tolerance was chosen empirically in pull request #111. The examples below compare the two choices of spectral gap.\n\nFor exact equations, the computed σ[r+1] is often below the machine precision. It is not a reliable estimate of the noise since the singular values are only computed with an error of about length(σ) * eps * σ[1], which is the default tolerance of LinearAlgebra.rank. For instance, for the equations x^2 + y^2 / 3 - 0.7 and x * y - y^3 / 7 at degree 6, the computed σ[r+1] / σ[1] changes from 1.0e-16 to 7.4e-17 between Julia v1.13 and v1.15, and the noise of the Gaussian elimination, about 1.9e-15, exceeds σ[r+1] / σ[r] · √n with v1.15. The level of noise used by accuracy is therefore at least length(σ) * eps * σ[1].","title":"Macaulay null spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/dependence_tolerance/#Moment-matrices-with-noise","page":"Tolerance of the numerical dependence of monomials","text":"We now consider the moment matrix of atoms lying on a variety so that some monomials are dependent before the last pivot. To mimic the inaccuracy of an SDP solver, the moment matrix is perturbed by a random symmetric matrix of relative norm δ. The rank is fixed to the number of atoms with FixedRank to separate the choice of the rank from the choice of the pivots. The atoms and the true pivots are computed in BigFloat so that roundoff in the reference pivots is small compared with the perturbation.\n\nfunction moments(vars, d, atoms, δ; case = nothing)\n    monos = monomials(vars, 0:d)\n    k = length(atoms)\n    V = [m(vars => a) for m in monos, a in atoms]\n    W = big.(range(1, 2; length = k))\n    exact = Float64.(V * Diagonal(W) * V')\n    piv = true_pivots(Matrix(transpose(V)))\n    E = randn(size(exact))\n    E = (E + E') / 2\n    M = exact + δ * opnorm(exact) / opnorm(E) * E\n    chol = MM.low_rank_ldlt(M, MM.SVDLDLT(), MM.FixedRank(k))\n    noise_level = @sprintf(\"%.0e\", δ)\n    labels = isnothing(case) ? (noise_level,) : (case, noise_level)\n    return row(\n        labels,\n        chol.L,\n        MB.SubBasis{MB.Monomial}(monos),\n        chol.singular_values,\n        k,\n        piv,\n    )\nend\n\n# Atoms on the circle of radius `radius` with angles `2π i / k + 0.1 i^2` for\n# `i = 1, ..., k`\nfunction circle(radius, k)\n    return [\n        radius .* [cos(t), sin(t)] for\n        t in (2big(π) * i / k + big(0.1) * i^2 for i in 1:k)\n    ]\nend\nfunction sphere()\n    u, v = big(π) * rand() - big(π) / 2, 2big(π) * rand()\n    return [cos(u) * cos(v), cos(u) * sin(v), sin(u)]\nend\nsphere (generic function with 1 method)\n\nAll three cases use monomials through degree four. C₁ and C₃ each have seven atoms on a circle of radius one and three, respectively. S has twelve atoms on the unit sphere. Each row gives the relative perturbation δ for that case.\n\nRandom.seed!(0)\ncircle_atoms = circle(1, 7)\nlarge_circle_atoms = circle(3, 7)\nsphere_atoms = [sphere() for _ in 1:12]\ntable(\n    [\n        [\n            moments([x, y], 4, circle_atoms, δ; case = \"C₁\") for\n            δ in [1e-12, 1e-8, 1e-6, 1e-4]\n        ]\n        [\n            moments([x, y], 4, large_circle_atoms, δ; case = \"C₃\") for\n            δ in [1e-12, 1e-8, 1e-6]\n        ]\n        [\n            moments([x, y, z], 4, sphere_atoms, δ; case = \"S\") for\n            δ in [1e-12, 1e-8, 1e-6, 1e-4]\n        ]\n    ],\n    (\"Case\", \"δ\"),\n)\n  Case     δ  n      ρ₁      ρᵣ   Noise  Signal\n  –––– ––––– –– ––––––– ––––––– ––––––– –––––––\n    C₁ 1e-12 15 7.9e-13 8.7e-11 4.1e-12 4.1e-01\n    C₁ 1e-08 15 7.1e-09 7.8e-07 7.6e-08 4.1e-01\n    C₁ 1e-06 15 7.4e-07 8.2e-05 1.4e-05 4.1e-01\n    C₁ 1e-04 15 6.5e-05 7.2e-03 3.2e-03 4.1e-01\n    C₃ 1e-12 15 8.9e-13 1.1e-10 5.6e-11 1.9e-02\n    C₃ 1e-08 15 8.2e-09 1.0e-06 3.1e-07 1.9e-02\n    C₃ 1e-06 15 8.4e-07 1.1e-04 5.6e-05 1.9e-02\n     S 1e-12 35 8.7e-13 2.1e-09 6.7e-10 1.1e-01\n     S 1e-08 35 8.4e-09 2.0e-05 2.4e-06 1.1e-01\n     S 1e-06 35 8.1e-07 2.0e-03 7.5e-04 1.1e-01\n     S 1e-04 35 8.4e-05 2.1e-01 1.3e-02 1.1e-01\n\n  \u001b[1mRecovery of the reference pivots\u001b[22m\n\n  Case     δ ρ₁/√n ρᵣ·√n Gap at r First gap\n  –––– ––––– ––––– ––––– –––––––– –––––––––\n    C₁ 1e-12     ✗     ✓        ✓         ✓\n    C₁ 1e-08     ✗     ✓        ✓         ✓\n    C₁ 1e-06     ✗     ✓        ✓         ✓\n    C₁ 1e-04     ✗     ✓        ✓         ✓\n    C₃ 1e-12     ✗     ✓        ✓         ✓\n    C₃ 1e-08     ✗     ✓        ✓         ✓\n    C₃ 1e-06     ✗     ✓        ✓         ✓\n     S 1e-12     ✗     ✓        ✓         ✓\n     S 1e-08     ✗     ✓        ✓         ✓\n     S 1e-06     ✗     ✓        ✓         ✓\n     S 1e-04     ✗     ✗        ✗         ✗\n\nOn the circle, x² is dependent on 1 and y². A tolerance below the measured noise can select this column as a pivot. Several rows illustrate this failure for the first tolerance. The last row is discussed below.","title":"Moment matrices with noise"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/dependence_tolerance/#Moment-matrices-without-gap","page":"Tolerance of the numerical dependence of monomials","text":"Should we then simply use σ[r+1] / σ[r] ? Consider the following moment matrix obtained by an SDP solver for (Legat et al., 2020; Example 3.9).\n\n@polyvar a b\nν = MM.moment_matrix(\n    [\n        14.8107 3.37426 0.769196 0.175447 0.0400656 0.00917416 0.00211654\n        3.37426 0.769196 0.175447 0.0400656 0.00917416 0.00211654 0.000498924\n        0.769196 0.175447 0.0400656 0.00917416 0.00211654 0.000498924 0.000125543\n        0.175447 0.0400656 0.00917416 0.00211654 0.000498924 0.000125543 3.76638e-5\n        0.0400656 0.00917416 0.00211654 0.000498924 0.000125543 3.76638e-5 1.62883e-5\n        0.00917416 0.00211654 0.000498924 0.000125543 3.76638e-5 1.62883e-5 1.07817e-5\n        0.00211654 0.000498924 0.000125543 3.76638e-5 1.62883e-5 1.07817e-5 1.10658e-5\n    ][\n        7:-1:1,\n        7:-1:1,\n    ],\n    monomials([a, b], 6),\n)\nF = svd(Matrix(MM.value_matrix(ν)))\nσ = F.S\nσ / σ[1]\n7-element Vector{Float64}:\n 1.0\n 3.5538107943662456e-5\n 3.4104829982664462e-6\n 5.222188174031103e-7\n 1.0517838334889796e-7\n 1.866938741822846e-8\n 7.63109391039627e-9\n\nAfter the first singular value the spectrum decays gradually. The table compares the extracted atoms when we set the accuracy to σ[r+1] / σ[r] and when we use the package default. The last two columns give the atom ratios b/a for these two choices. Both use the same computed vectors and the Echelon solver.\n\nfunction extract(null)\n    atoms = MM.solve(null, MM.Echelon())\n    if isnothing(atoms) || isempty(atoms)\n        return \"none\"\n    end\n    return join([@sprintf(\"%.4f\", atom[2] / atom[1]) for atom in atoms], \", \")\nend\n\nrows = map([1e-4, 1e-5, 1e-6, 1e-7, 1e-8]) do rank_tol\n    check = MM.LeadingRelativeRankTol(rank_tol)\n    r = MM.rank_from_singular_values(σ, check)\n    Z = F.U[:, 1:r]\n    adjacent = MM.MacaulayNullspace(Z, ν.basis, σ[r+1] / σ[r])\n    package = MM.MacaulayNullspace(Z, ν.basis, MM.accuracy(σ, r, check))\n    return @sprintf(\n        \"| %.0e | %d | %.1e | %.1e | %s | %s |\",\n        rank_tol,\n        r,\n        σ[r+1] / σ[1],\n        σ[r+1] / σ[r],\n        extract(adjacent),\n        extract(package),\n    )\nend\n\nMarkdown.parse(\n    join(\n        [\n            \"| Rank tol. | r | ρ₁ | ρᵣ | b/a (ρᵣ) | b/a (default) |\"\n            \"| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\"\n            rows\n        ],\n        \"\\n\",\n    ),\n)\n  Rank tol. r      ρ₁      ρᵣ       b/a (ρᵣ)                                    b/a (default)\n  ––––––––– – ––––––– ––––––– –––––––––––––– ––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––\n      1e-04 1 3.6e-05 3.6e-05         0.2278                                           0.2278\n      1e-05 2 3.4e-06 9.6e-02 0.5021, 0.0000                                           0.2278\n      1e-06 3 5.2e-07 1.5e-01         0.0000                                           0.2279\n      1e-07 5 1.9e-08 1.8e-01         0.0000                                           0.2279\n      1e-08 6 7.6e-09 4.1e-01    0.0000, Inf -0.2809, 0.1234, 0.2280, 0.5483, 0.9916, 11.1524\n\nThe dominant atom has b / a ≈ 0.2278. Lowering the rank tolerance retains additional directions and makes the choice of pivots relevant to which atoms can be extracted.","title":"Moment matrices without gap"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/dependence_tolerance/#Choosing-which-gap-to-use","page":"Tolerance of the numerical dependence of monomials","text":"GapBasedAccuracy wraps a rank check. With gap_tol = 1e-2, a gap after j means σ[j+1] < 10⁻² σ[j]. The default, search_later = false, checks only j = r. Setting search_later = true uses the first qualifying gap at or after r. If either policy finds a gap, it divides σ[j+1] by σ[r]. Otherwise it divides σ[r+1] by σ[1]. The numerator includes the roundoff floor described above. A later gap can suggest a noise floor for a larger signal space. Wedin's separation for the chosen subspace is still the separation at rank r. Changing the search range can even increase the tolerance. For example, with σ = [1, 1e-3, 5e-4, 4e-6] and r = 2, the default gives 5e-4. Searching one step further gives 4e-6 / 1e-3 = 4e-3.\n\nA. Spurious coordinate. In the diagonal matrix below, the clean rank-two subspace is spanned by coordinate vectors 1 and 3. A small perturbation couples directions 2 and 3 while leaving the fourth singular value zero.\n\nA = diagm([1.0, 5e-4, 1e-3, 0.0])\nA[2, 3] = A[3, 2] = 1e-12\nF_coupled = svd(A)\nZ_coupled = F_coupled.U[:, 1:2]\nbasis_coupled = MB.SubBasis{MB.Monomial}(monomials([x], 0:3))\ncoupled_pivots = gap_pivots(Z_coupled, basis_coupled, F_coupled.S, 2)\n@assert coupled_pivots == (Set([1, 3]), Set([1, 2]))\n\nThe selected-rank policy skips the spurious 2e-9 component in column 2. Searching for a later gap finds one after the third singular value and sets a tolerance of about 1.8e-12, which accepts that component.\n\nB. Genuine coordinate. The following moment matrix comes from the package's atom-extraction tests. Its measure has atoms at (1, 3) and (4, 3) with approximate weights 0.864 and 0.136. We retain one direction of this two-atom example. The clean moment matrix has strictly positive entries, so its dominant eigenvector can be chosen strictly positive. Its first coordinate, the constant monomial, is a genuine pivot.\n\nQ = [\n    1.0000000000000002 2.9999999777389235 1.408602019734018 8.999999911981313 4.225805987406138 3.043010098670093\n    2.9999999777389235 8.999999911981313 4.225805987406138 26.999999824988187 12.677417999642149 9.129030026074997\n    1.408602019734018 4.225805987406138 3.043010098670093 12.677417999642149 9.129030026074997 9.580642414414385\n    8.999999911981313 26.999999824988187 12.677417999642149 80.99999955990646 38.03225428611012 27.387090350285558\n    4.225805987406138 12.677417999642149 9.129030026074997 38.03225428611012 27.387090350285558 28.74192618075039\n    3.043010098670093 9.129030026074997 9.580642414414385 27.387090350285558 28.74192618075039 35.73117167739159\n]\nF_atoms = svd(Q)\nZ_atoms = F_atoms.U[:, 1:1]\nbasis_atoms = MB.SubBasis{MB.Monomial}(monomials([x, y], 0:2))\natom_pivots = gap_pivots(Z_atoms, basis_atoms, F_atoms.S, 1)\n@assert atom_pivots == (Set([4]), Set([1]))\n\ntable([\n    row((\"A\",), Z_coupled, basis_coupled, F_coupled.S, 2, Set([1, 3])),\n    row((\"B\",), Z_atoms, basis_atoms, F_atoms.S, 1, Set([1])),\n])\n  Case n      ρ₁      ρᵣ   Noise  Signal\n  –––– – ––––––– ––––––– ––––––– –––––––\n     A 4 5.0e-04 5.0e-01 2.0e-09 1.0e+00\n     B 6 2.1e-01 2.1e-01 0.0e+00 8.3e-02\n\n  \u001b[1mRecovery of the reference pivots\u001b[22m\n\n  Case ρ₁/√n ρᵣ·√n Gap at r First gap\n  –––– ––––– ––––– –––––––– –––––––––\n     A     ✓     ✗        ✓         ✗\n     B     ✗     ✗        ✗         ✓\n\nThe second singular value is about 0.21 σ[1], well above the residual singular values from the third onward. The selected-rank fallback sets a tolerance of about 0.51, skipping the genuine first coordinate of magnitude 0.083. The gap after the second direction gives a tolerance of about 9.5e-9 and recovers the constant monomial. To use this policy when computing the image space, pass it as the rank check.\n\nmeasure_moments = MM.moment_matrix(Q, monomials([x, y], 0:2))\npolicy = MM.GapBasedAccuracy(MM.FixedRank(1); search_later = true)\nimage = MM.image_space(measure_moments, policy)\nd = MM.BasisDependence{MM.LinearDependence}(image)\n@assert findall(!MM.is_dependent, d.dependence) == [1]\n\nThe cutoff controls which gaps qualify. Increasing it to 0.25 makes the gap after the first singular value qualify for both policies.\n\n@assert gap_pivots(Z_atoms, basis_atoms, F_atoms.S, 1; gap_tol = 0.25) ==\n        (Set([4]), Set([4]))\n\nA later gap can help when discarded directions still carry signal, as in the two-atom example. A gap far below the selected rank can also suggest a tolerance small enough to admit roundoff or perturbation in a dependent column, as in the coupled matrix. The clean pivots distinguish these two outcomes even though both policies recover the requested number of pivots.","title":"Choosing which gap to use"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/dependence_tolerance/#Limitations","page":"Tolerance of the numerical dependence of monomials","text":"Consider six atoms on the twisted cubic, two of which are very close. We use monomials through degree three and vary the relative perturbation δ.\n\ntwisted_cubic_atoms =\n    [[t, t^2, t^3] for t in big.([-0.9, -0.4, 0.1, 0.5, 0.5001, 0.95])]\ntable(\n    [\n        moments([x, y, z], 3, twisted_cubic_atoms, δ) for\n        δ in [1e-12, 1e-8, 1e-6]\n    ],\n    (\"δ\",),\n)\n      δ  n      ρ₁      ρᵣ   Noise  Signal\n  ––––– –– ––––––– ––––––– ––––––– –––––––\n  1e-12 20 7.9e-13 1.4e-02 0.0e+00 3.4e-01\n  1e-08 20 8.0e-09 8.8e-01 0.0e+00 3.1e-01\n  1e-06 20 6.5e-07 9.3e-01 0.0e+00 1.1e-01\n\n  \u001b[1mRecovery of the reference pivots\u001b[22m\n\n      δ ρ₁/√n ρᵣ·√n Gap at r First gap\n  ––––– ––––– ––––– –––––––– –––––––––\n  1e-12     ✓     ✓        ✓         ✓\n  1e-08     ✓     ✗        ✓         ✓\n  1e-06     ✓     ✗        ✓         ✓\n\nThe first six monomials are already independent. A generic six-dimensional subspace has the same six pivots, so a check mark here says little about recovery of the clean subspace. At higher perturbations the sixth singular direction can be poorly determined.\n\nThe gap threshold 10⁻² is heuristic. For the sphere at δ = 1e-4, the fallback can accept spurious pivots. The noise and signal columns show the interval of tolerances that recovers the reference pivots. Once the measured noise exceeds the signal, no single tolerance along this elimination path can recover them all.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"moments/#Moments-and-expectation","page":"Moments and expectation","text":"","title":"Moments and expectation"},{"category":"section","location":"moments/#Moment","page":"Moments and expectation","text":"Given a measure mu and a monomial m, the moment m of the measure is defined by the expectation mathbbE_mum. Given a monomial and a value for the moment, a moment can be created using the moment function\n\nThe moment function returns an AbstractMoment which is a subtype of AbstractMomentLike. An AbstractMomentLike is a type that can act like an AbstractMoment (it is similar to MultivariatePolynomials' AbstractMonomialLike, AbstractTermLike and AbstractPolynomialLike), that is, it implements the following two functions","title":"Moment"},{"category":"section","location":"moments/#Measure","page":"Moments and expectation","text":"Given a monomials and a values for the moments, a \"moment vector\" can be created using the moment_vector function\n\nThe moment_vector function returns an AbstractMeasure which is a subtype of AbstractMeasureLike. Note that it does not actually compute the probability density function of a measure having these moments, it simply stores a vector of moments belonging to a hypothetical measure. However, it acts like a measure when taking its scalar product with a polynomial.\n\nAn AbstractMeasureLike is a type that can act like an AbstractMeasure, that is, it implements the following two functions\n\nThe moments of the dirac measure for a vector of monomials can be obtained by the dirac function","title":"Measure"},{"category":"section","location":"moments/#Expectation","page":"Moments and expectation","text":"The expectation of polynomial with respect to a measure can be computed either using MultivariateMoments.expectation or using the Base.dot scalar product.","title":"Expectation"},{"category":"section","location":"moments/#Quotient","page":"Moments and expectation","text":"The moments of a measure supported on an algebraic set are only determined modulo the ideal of the set. The quotient of the span of monomials modulo this ideal is computed by quotient_basis.","title":"Quotient"},{"category":"function","location":"moments/#MultivariateMoments.moment","page":"Moments and expectation","text":"moment(α, p)\n\nCreates the moment of the polynomial p of value α.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.moment"},{"category":"function","location":"moments/#MultivariateMoments.moment_value","page":"Moments and expectation","text":"moment_value(m::AbstractMomentLike)\n\nReturns the value of the moment m.\n\nExamples\n\nCalling moment_value(moment(3.1, x*y^2)) should return 3.1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.moment_value"},{"category":"function","location":"moments/#MultivariateMoments.moment_vector","page":"Moments and expectation","text":"moment_vector(values::AbstractVector{T}, monos::AbstractVector{<:AbstractMonomial}; rtol=Base.rtoldefault(T), atol=zero(T))\n\nCreates a measure with moments moment(values[i], monos[i]) for each i. An error is thrown if there exists i and j such that monos[i] == monos[j] but !isapprox(values[i], values[j]; rtol=rtol, atol=atol).\n\n\n\n\n\nmoment_vector(values::AbstractVector, basis::MB.QuotientBasis)\n\nReturn the moments of the elements of basis.basis given the values values of the linear functional on the elements of basis. As the coefficients in basis are basis.matrix times the coefficients in basis.basis, the moments are obtained with the adjoint map transpose(basis.matrix) * values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.moment_vector"},{"category":"method","location":"moments/#MultivariatePolynomials.variables-Tuple{MomentVector}","page":"Moments and expectation","text":"variables(μ::MomentVector)\n\nReturns the variables of μ in decreasing order. Just like in MultivariatePolynomials, it could contain variables of zero degree in every monomial.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariatePolynomials.variables"},{"category":"method","location":"moments/#MultivariatePolynomials.maxdegree-Tuple{MomentVector}","page":"Moments and expectation","text":"maxdegree(μ::AbstractMeasureLike)\n\nReturns the maximal degree of the monomials of μ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariatePolynomials.maxdegree"},{"category":"method","location":"moments/#MultivariatePolynomials.mindegree-Tuple{MomentVector}","page":"Moments and expectation","text":"mindegree(μ::MomentVector)\n\nReturns the minimal degree of the monomials of μ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariatePolynomials.mindegree"},{"category":"method","location":"moments/#MultivariatePolynomials.extdegree-Tuple{MomentVector}","page":"Moments and expectation","text":"extdegree(μ::MomentVector)\n\nReturns the extremal degrees of the monomials of μ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariatePolynomials.extdegree"},{"category":"function","location":"moments/#MultivariateMoments.moments","page":"Moments and expectation","text":"moments(μ::MomentVector)\n\nReturns an iterator over the moments of μ sorted in decreasing order of monomial.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.moments"},{"category":"function","location":"moments/#MultivariateMoments.dirac","page":"Moments and expectation","text":"dirac(X::AbstractVector{<:AbstractMoment}, s::AbstractSubstitution...)\n\nCreates the dirac measure by evaluating the moments of X using s.\n\nExamples\n\nCalling dirac([x*y, x*y^2], x=>3, y=>2) should the measure with moment x*y of value 6 and moment x*y^2 of value 12.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.dirac"},{"category":"function","location":"moments/#MultivariateMoments.expectation","page":"Moments and expectation","text":"MultivariateMoments.expectation(μ::AbstractMeasureLike, p::AbstractPolynomialLike)\nMultivariateMoments.expectation(p::AbstractPolynomialLike, μ::AbstractMeasureLike)\n\nComputes the expectation mathbbE_mup.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.expectation"},{"category":"function","location":"moments/#LinearAlgebra.dot","page":"Moments and expectation","text":"dot(μ::AbstractMeasureLike, p::AbstractPolynomialLike)\ndot(p::AbstractPolynomialLike, μ::AbstractMeasureLike)\n\nSee expectation\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.dot"},{"category":"function","location":"moments/#MultivariateMoments.quotient_basis","page":"Moments and expectation","text":"quotient_basis(basis::MB.SubBasis{MB.Monomial}, domain)\n\nReturn a MultivariateBases.QuotientBasis representing the quotient of the span of basis modulo the ideal of domain, which is either an algebraic set, a polynomial ideal or a SemialgebraicSets.TruncatedIdeal. The matrix is a sparse matrix with float of the coefficient type of domain as element type.\n\nIf a Gröbner basis of the ideal is known, the matrix gives the remainders of the elements of basis modulo the Gröbner basis. For a truncated ideal, the polynomials whose coefficients are in the kernel of the matrix are the polynomials in the row space of the Macaulay matrix of its degree. If domain is an AlgebraicSet whose Gröbner basis is not known, it is not computed and the ideal is instead truncated at the maximal degree of basis, see SemialgebraicSets.truncated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.quotient_basis"},{"category":"section","location":"atoms/#Atoms-extration","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"","title":"Atoms extration"},{"category":"section","location":"atoms/#Vectorized-matrix","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"","title":"Vectorized matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"atoms/#Moment-matrix","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"","title":"Moment matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"atoms/#Atomic-measure","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"","title":"Atomic measure"},{"category":"section","location":"atoms/#Atoms-extraction","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"Given a MomentMatrix containing the moments of an atomic measure, atomic_measure attempts to recover the dirac centers and weights by first computing an algebraic system with the atom centers as solution with compute_support!.\n\nFor the first step of compute_support!, there are two approaches. The first one is to exploit the flat extension to directly obtain the multiplication matrices.\n\nThe second approach is to first obtain the image space of the moment matrix, represented as a MacaulayNullspace and to then compute the multiplication matrices from this image space. This is implemented by the ImageSpaceSolver.\n\nThis image space is obtained from a low rank LDLT decomposition of the moment matrix. This decomposition can either be obtained by a cholesky or SVD decomposition from which we remove the rows corresponding to the negligeable eigen/singular values.\n\nThe choice of cutoff between the significant and negligeable eigen/singular values is parametrized by the following interface:\n\nThe rank check can be chosen among the following:\n\nGiven the MacaulayNullspace, there are two approaches implemented to obtain the moment matrices:\n\nThe Echelon uses the RowEchelon package to determine the independent rows (which is not numerically stable) while the ShiftNullspace uses RankChecks with the singular values so it should have better numerical behavior. They can either simply distinguish the dependency of rows with LinearDependence or use a sieve with StaircaseDependence to save some the computation of the singular values for some submatrices:\n\nThe relationship between the dependent and the independent rows are then stored in a BorderBasis. By default, calling solve with only a BorderBasisas argument or providing aSemialgebraicSets.AbstractMultiplicationMatricesSolveras second argument will try to construct the moment matrices from these and find the solutions from these moment matrices. Alternatively, give an [AlgebraicBorderSolver](@ref) or a [AlgebraicFallbackBorderSolver`](@ref) as second argument to solve the system formed by these relations with these instead.\n\nOnce the center of the atoms are determined, a linear system is solved to determine the weights corresponding to each dirac. By default, MomentMatrixWeightSolver is used by atomic_measure so that if there are small differences between moment values corresponding to the same monomial in the matrix (which can happen if these moments were computed numerically by a semidefinite proramming solvers, e.g., with SumOfSquares), the linear system handles that automatically.","title":"Atoms extraction"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.SymMatrix","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct SymMatrix{T} <: AbstractMatrix{T}\n    Q::Vector{T}\n    n::Int\nend\n\nSymmetric n times n matrix storing the vectorized upper triangular part of the matrix in the Q vector (this corresponds to the vectorized form of MathOptInterface.PositiveSemidefiniteConeTriangle). It implement the AbstractMatrix interface except for setindex! as it might break its symmetry. The symmetric_setindex! function should be used instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.SymMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.VectorizedHermitianMatrix","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct VectorizedHermitianMatrix{T} <: AbstractMatrix{Complex{T}}\n    Q::Vector{T}\n    n::Int\nend\n\nHermitian n times n matrix storing the vectorized upper triangular real part of the matrix followed by the vectorized upper triangular imaginary part in the Q vector (this corresponds to the vectorized form of ComplexOptInterface.HermitianPositiveSemidefiniteConeTriangle). It implement the AbstractMatrix interface except for setindex! as it might break its symmetry. The symmetric_setindex! function should be used instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.VectorizedHermitianMatrix"},{"category":"function","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.square_getindex","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"square_getindex!(Q::SymMatrix, I)\n\nReturn the SymMatrix corresponding to Q[I, I].\n\n\n\n\n\nsquare_getindex!(Q::VectorizedHermitianMatrix, I)\n\nReturn the VectorizedHermitianMatrix corresponding to Q[I, I].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.square_getindex"},{"category":"function","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.symmetric_setindex!","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"symmetric_setindex!(Q::SymMatrix, value, i::Integer, j::Integer)\n\nSet Q[i, j] and Q[j, i] to the value value.\n\n\n\n\n\nsymmetric_setindex!(Q::VectorizedHermitianMatrix, value, i::Integer, j::Integer)\n\nSet Q[i, j] to the value value and Q[j, i] to the value -value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.symmetric_setindex!"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.MomentMatrix","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"mutable struct MomentMatrix{T,B<:SA.ExplicitBasis,MT<:AbstractMatrix{T}} <:\n            AbstractMomentMatrix{T,B}\n    Q::MT\n    basis::B\n    support::Union{Nothing,AbstractAlgebraicSet}\nend\n\nMeasure nu represented by the moments of the monomial matrix x x^top in the symmetric matrix Q. The set of points that are zeros of all the polynomials p such that mathbbE_nup = 0 is stored in the field support when it is computed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.MomentMatrix"},{"category":"function","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.moment_matrix","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"moment_matrix(μ::MomentVector, x)\n\nCreates a matrix the moment matrix for the moment matrix  x x^top using the moments of μ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.moment_matrix"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.WeightedDiracMeasure","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct WeightedDiracMeasure{T}\n    center::Vector{T}\n    weight::T\nend\n\nRepresents a weighted dirac measure with centered at center and with weight weight.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.WeightedDiracMeasure"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.AtomicMeasure","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct AtomicMeasure{T, AT, V} <: AbstractMeasureLike{T}\n    variables::V                           # Vector/Tuple of variables\n    atoms::Vector{WeightedDiracMeasure{T, AT}} # Atoms of the measure\nend\n\nAn measure is said to be atomic if it is a sum of weighted dirac measures. For instance, eta = 2 delta_(1 0) + 3 delta_(12 12) is an atomic measure since it is a sum of the diracs centered at (1 0) and (12 12) and weighted respectively by 2 and 3. That is, mathbbE_etap = 2p(1 0) + 3p(12 12).\n\nThe AtomicMeasure struct stores an atomic measure where variables contains the variables and atoms contains atoms of the measure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.AtomicMeasure"},{"category":"function","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.compute_support!","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"function compute_support!(ν::MomentMatrix, rank_check, solver) end\n\nComputes the support field of ν wth solver using a low-rank decomposition with the rank evaluated with rank_check.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.compute_support!"},{"category":"function","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.atomic_measure","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"atomic_measure(ν::MomentMatrix, rank_check::RankCheck, [dec::LowRankLDLTAlgorithm], [solver::SemialgebraicSets.AbstractAlgebraicSolver])\n\nReturn an AtomicMeasure with the atoms of ν if it is atomic or nothing if ν is not atomic. The rank_check and dec parameters are passed as is to the low_rank_ldlt function. By default, dec is an instance of SVDLDLT. The extraction relies on the solution of a system of algebraic equations. using solver. For instance, given a MomentMatrix, μ, the following extract atoms using a rank_check of 1e-4 for the low-rank decomposition and homotopy continuation to solve the obtained system of algebraic equations.\n\nusing HomotopyContinuation\nsolver = SemialgebraicSetsHCSolver(; compile = false)\natoms = atomic_measure(ν, 1e-4, solver)\n\nIf no solver is given, the default solver of SemialgebraicSets is used which currently computes the Gröbner basis, then the multiplication matrices and then the Schur decomposition of a random combination of these matrices. For floating point arithmetics, homotopy continuation is recommended as it is more numerically stable than Gröbner basis computation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.atomic_measure"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.FlatExtension","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct FlatExtension{\n    MMS<:SemialgebraicSets.AbstractMultiplicationMatricesSolver,\n}\n    multiplication_matrices_solver::MMS\nend\n\nGiven a moment matrix satisfying the flat extension property described in [L09, Section 5.3], computes the multiplication matrices using the formula given in [L09, Lemma 6.21] or [LLR08, Section 4.4.4]. Given the multiplication matrices, the solutions are obtained with multiplication_matrices_solver.\n\n[L09] Laurent, Monique. Sums of squares, moment matrices and optimization over polynomials. Emerging applications of algebraic geometry (2009): 157-270.\n\n[LLR08] Lasserre, Jean Bernard and Laurent, Monique, and Rostalski, Philipp. Semidefinite characterization and computation of zero-dimensional real radical ideals. Foundations of Computational Mathematics 8 (2008): 607-647.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.FlatExtension"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.MacaulayNullspace","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct MacaulayNullspace{T,MT<:AbstractMatrix{T},BT}\n    matrix::MT\n    basis::BT\n    accuracy::T\nend\n\nThis matrix with rows indexed by basis can either be interpreted as the right null space of a Macaulay matrix with columns indexed by basis (resp. or the image space of a moment matrix with rows and columns indexed by basis). The value of matrix[i, j] should be interpreted as the value of the ith element of basis for the jth generator of the null space (resp. image) space. The SVD constructors produce orthonormal columns. Cholesky factors and restrictions to subsets of the rows can have other column norms. accuracy supplies a relative tolerance for dependence calculations, see accuracy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.MacaulayNullspace"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.ImageSpaceSolver","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct ImageSpaceSolver{A<:LowRankLDLTAlgorithm,S<:MacaulayNullspaceSolver}\n    ldlt::A\n    null::S\nend\n\nComputes the image space of the moment matrix using ldlt and then solve it with null.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.ImageSpaceSolver"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.LowRankLDLTAlgorithm","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"LowRankLDLTAlgorithm\n\nMethod for computing a r times n matrix U of a n times n rank r psd matrix Q such that Q = U'U.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.LowRankLDLTAlgorithm"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.ShiftCholeskyLDLT","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"ShiftCholeskyLDLT <: LowRankLDLTAlgorithm\n\nShift the matrix by shift times the identity matrix before cholesky.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.ShiftCholeskyLDLT"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.AbsCholeskyLDLT","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"AbsCholeskyLDLT <: LowRankLDLTAlgorithm\n\nUse the modified Cholesky decomposition from PositiveFactorizations.jl. Negative pivots are replaced by their absolute values and zero pivots are discarded before applying the rank check. Unlike ShiftCholeskyLDLT, this does not require choosing a diagonal shift.\n\nRequires loading PositiveFactorizations with using PositiveFactorizations. Only real symmetric matrices are supported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.AbsCholeskyLDLT"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.SVDLDLT","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"SVDLDLT <: LowRankLDLTAlgorithm\n\nUse SVD decomposition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.SVDLDLT"},{"category":"function","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.low_rank_ldlt","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"MultivariateMoments.low_rank_ldlt(Q::AbstractMatrix, dec::LowRankLDLTAlgorithm, ranktol)\n\nReturns a r times n matrix U of a n times n rank r positive semidefinite matrix Q such that Q = U^top U. The rank of Q is the number of singular values larger than ranktol cdot sigma_1 where sigma_1 is the largest singular value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.low_rank_ldlt"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.LowRankLDLT","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct LowRankLDLT{T,Tr,C<:RankCheck}\n    L::Matrix{T}\n    singular_values::Vector{Tr}\n    rank_check::C\nend\n\nLow-Rank cholesky decomposition L * Diagonal(singular_values) * L' of size (n, r) of a n x n matrix with singular values singular_values[1] > ... > singular_values[n]. The rank was chosen given singular_values using rank_check via the rank_from_singular_values function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.LowRankLDLT"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.RankCheck","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"abstract type RankCheck end\n\nMethod for computing the rank with rank_from_singular_values based on a list of singular values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.RankCheck"},{"category":"function","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.rank_from_singular_values","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"rank_from_singular_values(σ, check::RankCheck)\n\nReturn the rank of a matrix with singular values σ (in decreasing order) using check.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.rank_from_singular_values"},{"category":"function","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.accuracy","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"accuracy(σ, r, check::RankCheck)\n\nReturn a relative tolerance for dependence calculations after selecting rank r from the decreasing singular values σ.\n\nBy default, check for a gap at r. Use GapBasedAccuracy to configure the threshold and search range. For a gap after σ[j], divide σ[j+1] by the weakest retained singular value σ[r]. If no gap is found, divide σ[r+1] by σ[1]. In both cases, first bound the numerator from below by length(σ) * eps * σ[1] to account for roundoff in the singular values. These ratios are heuristics for selecting pivots. Estimating a principal angle also requires information about the perturbation.\n\nFor rank zero return zero. For full rank use the default for check. See Tolerance of the numerical dependence of monomials for examples.\n\n\n\n\n\naccuracy(chol::LowRankLDLT)\n\nReturn the dependence tolerance derived from the retained rank and the spectrum stored by chol, see accuracy. Cholesky-based factorizations use the sorted squared diagonal pivots.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.accuracy"},{"category":"function","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.doubt","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"doubt(σ, check::RankCheck)\n\nReturns a value measuring the doubt of the rank check check. Lower values means more doubt so less certainty. This is used by FallbackRank for deciding whether the fallback should be used.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.doubt"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.UserRank","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct UserRank <: RankCheck\n    pagesize::Int\nend\n\nThe user chooses the rank given the singular values in a REPL.TerminalMenus.RadioMenu of page size pagesize.\n\nExample\n\njulia> rank_from_singular_values([1, 1e-1, 5e-2, 1e-5, 5e-6], UserRank())\nChoose the last significant singular value:\n   1.0\n   0.1\n > 0.05\n   1.0e-5\n   5.0e-6\n3\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.UserRank"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.FixedRank","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct FixedRank <: RankCheck\n    r::Int\nend\n\nThe rank is hardcoded to r, independently of the singular values.\n\nExample\n\njulia> rank_from_singular_values([1, 1e-1, 5e-2, 1e-5, 5e-6], FixedRank(3))\n3\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.FixedRank"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.FixedRanks","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"mutable struct FixedRanks <: RankCheck\n    r::Vector{Int}\n    current::Int\nend\n\nThe ith rank is hardcoded to r[i], independently of the singular values. The field current indicates how many ranks have already been asked. When current is length(r), no rank can be asked anymore.\n\nExample\n\njulia> check = FixedRanks([2, 3])\nFixedRanks([2, 3], 0)\n\njulia> rank_from_singular_values([1, 1e-1, 5e-5, 1e-5, 5e-6], check)\n2\n\njulia> rank_from_singular_values([1, 1e-1, 5e-2, 1e-5, 5e-6], check)\n3\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.FixedRanks"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.AbsoluteRankTol","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct AbsoluteRankTol{T} <: RankCheck\n    tol::T\nend\n\nThe rank is the number of singular values that are strictly larger than tol.\n\nExample\n\njulia> rank_from_singular_values([1, 1e-1, 5e-2, 1e-5, 5e-6], AbsoluteRankTol(1e-4))\n3\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.AbsoluteRankTol"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.LeadingRelativeRankTol","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct LeadingRelativeRankTol{T} <: RankCheck\n    tol::T\nend\n\nThe rank is the number of singular values that are strictly larger than tol * maximum(σ) where maximum(σ) is the largest singular value.\n\nExample\n\nWhen the matrix is obtained from a homogeneous problem where the scaling is irrelevant, LeadingRelativeRankTol may be preferable to AbsoluteRankTol as shown below\n\njulia> rank_from_singular_values(1e6 * [1, 1e-1, 5e-2, 1e-5, 5e-6], AbsoluteRankTol(1e-4))\n5\n\njulia> rank_from_singular_values(1e6 * [1, 1e-1, 5e-2, 1e-5, 5e-6], LeadingRelativeRankTol(1e-4))\n3\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.LeadingRelativeRankTol"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.DifferentialRankTol","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct DifferentialRankTol{T} <: RankCheck\n    tol::T\nend\n\nThe rank is the number of singular values before a singular value (not included) is tol times the previous one (included).\n\nExample\n\nIt is sometimes difficult to figure out the tolerance to use in LeadingRelativeRankTol. For instance, choosing 1e-3 will consider 1e-3 in the example below as not part of the rank while DifferentialRankTol would include it because it is close to the previous singular value.\n\njulia> rank_from_singular_values([1, 1e-1, 5e-2, 1e-2, 5e-3, 1e-3, 1e-6, 5e-7], LeadingRelativeRankTol(1e-3))\n5\n\njulia> rank_from_singular_values([1, 1e-1, 5e-2, 1e-2, 5e-3, 1e-3, 1e-6, 5e-7], DifferentialRankTol(1e-2))\n6\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.DifferentialRankTol"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.LargestDifferentialRank","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct LargestDifferentialRank <: RankCheck\nend\n\nThe rank is the number of singular values until the singular value that has the largest ratio with the next singular value.\n\nExample\n\nIt is sometimes difficult to figure out the tolerance to use in DifferentialRankTol. For instance, choosing 1e-2 will consider 1e-2, 5e-2 and 1e-3 in the example below as not part of the rank while LargestDifferentialRank would include them because there is a largest gap later.\n\njulia> rank_from_singular_values([1, 1e-2, 5e-2, 1e-3, 1e-6, 5e-7], DifferentialRankTol(1e-2))\n1\n\njulia> rank_from_singular_values([1, 1e-2, 5e-2, 1e-3, 1e-6, 5e-7], LargestDifferentialRank())\n4\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.LargestDifferentialRank"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.FallbackRank","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct FallbackRank{T,D,F} <: RankCheck\n    tol::T\n    default::D\n    fallback::F\nend\n\nDefaults to checking the rank with default and falls back to fallback if the doubt is strictly larger than tol. By default, fallback is UserRank.\n\nExample\n\nThe advantage of UserRank is that the user get to see if the rank check is ambiguous and act accordingly. The downside is that it might be cumbersome if there are many rank checks to do. With FallbackRank, the user only has to sort out the tricky ones. In the example below, the first example is handled by LargestDifferentialRank. For the second one, the user sees that this is a tricky choice and can manually choose one of the two ranks, then see the result of the rest of his code using this value of the code and then choose the other rank and see the impact of this different choice.\n\njulia> check = FallbackRank(1e-1, LargestDifferentialRank())\nFallbackRank{Float64, LargestDifferentialRank, UserRank}(0.1, LargestDifferentialRank(), UserRank(8))\n\njulia> rank_from_singular_values([1, 1e-2, 5e-2, 1e-3, 1e-6, 5e-7], check)\n4\n\njulia> rank_from_singular_values([1, 1e-2, 5e-2, 1e-3, 5e-6, 5e-7], check)\nChoose the last significant singular value:\n > 1.0\n   0.01\n   0.05\n   0.001\n   5.0e-6\n   5.0e-7\n1\n\njulia> rank_from_singular_values([1, 1e-2, 5e-2, 1e-3, 5e-6, 5e-7], check)\nChoose the last significant singular value:\n   1.0\n   0.01\n   0.05\n > 0.001\n   5.0e-6\n   5.0e-7\n4\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.FallbackRank"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.GapBasedAccuracy","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"GapBasedAccuracy(check::RankCheck; gap_tol = 1e-2, search_later = false)\n\nSelect the rank using check and configure how accuracy scales the pivot tolerance. A gap after singular value σ[j] means σ[j+1] < gap_tol * σ[j].\n\nBy default only the gap at the selected rank r is considered. Set search_later = true to use the first gap at or after r. A later gap can identify a noise floor below discarded signal directions. Both the noise level and its normalization change, so the resulting tolerance can increase or decrease. See Tolerance of the numerical dependence of monomials for examples.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.GapBasedAccuracy"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.ShiftNullspace","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct ShiftNullspace{D,C<:RankCheck} <: MacaulayNullspaceSolver\n    check::C\nend\n\nFrom the MacaulayNullspace, computes multiplication matrices by exploiting the shift property of the rows [DBD12]. The rank check check is used to determine the standard monomials among the row indices of the null space.\n\n[DBD12] Dreesen, Philippe, Batselier, Kim, and De Moor, Bart. Back to the roots: Polynomial system solving, linear algebra, systems theory. IFAC Proceedings Volumes 45.16 (2012): 1203-1208.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.ShiftNullspace"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.Echelon","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct Echelon{D,S<:Union{Nothing,SemialgebraicSets.AbstractAlgebraicSolver}} <:\n    MacaulayNullspaceSolver\n    solver::S\nend\n\nGiven a MacaulayNullspace, computes its echelon form (corresponding to the Canonical Null Space of [D13]) with Gaussian elimination. From this echelon form, the left null space can easily be computed using using [HL05, (8)]. This left null space forms a system of polynomial equations.\n\nnote: Note\nIn the context of compute_support!, if the MacaulayNullspace was obtained using SVDLDLT, the left null space can easily be obtained from the singular vectors corresponding to the negligeable singular values that were removed. However, as mentioned [LLR08, Section 4.4.5], these will usually give an overdetermined bases. As shown in [S04, Section 10.8.2], it is desirable to avoid overdetermined bases because it could lead to inconsistencies in the basis for numerical reasons. For this reason, this method computes the left null space from the echelon form of the significant singular values instead.\n\nLet B be the set of monomials corresponding to the rows of the pivots of this echelon form.  If the moment matrix satisfies the flat extension property described in [L09, Section 5.3] then all monomials of the border of B (as defined in [LLR08, (2.3)]) will correspond to a row of of the matrix. In that case, the polynomial of the system obtained by [HL05, (8)] form a rewriting family for B [L09, (2.16)] a.k.a. a B-border prebasis (as defined in [LLR08, (2.4)]). Therefore, they can be used to compute multiplication matrices.\n\n[HL05] Henrion, D. & Lasserre, J-B. Detecting Global Optimality and Extracting Solutions of GloptiPoly 2005\n\n[D13] Dreesen, Philippe. Back to the Roots: Polynomial System Solving Using Linear Algebra Ph.D. thesis (2013)\n\n[L09] Laurent, Monique. Sums of squares, moment matrices and optimization over polynomials. Emerging applications of algebraic geometry (2009): 157-270.\n\n[LLR08] Lasserre, Jean Bernard and Laurent, Monique, and Rostalski, Philipp. Semidefinite characterization and computation of zero-dimensional real radical ideals. Foundations of Computational Mathematics 8 (2008): 607-647.\n\n[S04] Stetter, Hans J. Numerical polynomial algebra. Society for Industrial and Applied Mathematics, 2004.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.Echelon"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.LinearDependence","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"@enum LinearDependence INDEPENDENT TRIVIAL DEPENDENT\n\nLinear dependence of the element of a basis representing the indices of the rows of a [MacaulayNullspace]. DEPENDENT indicates that it is linearly dependent to rows that appear earlier in the basis. TRIVIAL indicates that the element was not in the original basis so it is trivially independent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.LinearDependence"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.StaircaseDependence","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct StaircaseDependence\n    standard_or_corner::Bool\n    linear::LinearDependence\nend\n\nDependence of the element of a basis representing the indices of the rows of a [MacaulayNullspace]. If the field standard_or_corner is true then the elements is either standard or is a corner (depending on the linear dependence encoded in the linear field). Otherwise, it is a border that is not a corner or it is not even a border.  See LinearDependence for the linear field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.StaircaseDependence"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.BasisDependence","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct BasisDependence{D,B<:SA.ExplicitBasis}\n    basis::B\n    dependence::Vector{D}\nend\n\nThe dependence between the elements of a basis.\n\ntip: Tip\nIf the number of variables is 2 or 3, it can be visualized with Plots.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.BasisDependence"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.BorderBasis","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct BorderBasis{D,T,MT<:AbstractMatrix{T},B}\n    dependence::BasisDependence{D,B}\n    matrix::MT\nend\n\nThis matrix with rows indexed by standard and columns indexed by border a standard-border basis of the ideal border .- matrix' * standard [LLR08, Section 2.5]. For solving this with a multiplication matrix solver, it is necessary for the basis border to be a superset of the set of corners of standard and it is sufficient for it to be the border of standard.\n\n[LLR08] Lasserre, Jean Bernard and Laurent, Monique, and Rostalski, Philipp. Semidefinite characterization and computation of zero-dimensional real radical ideals. Foundations of Computational Mathematics 8 (2008): 607-647.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.BorderBasis"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.AlgebraicBorderSolver","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"Base.@kwdef struct AlgebraicBorderSolver{\n    D,\n    A<:Union{Nothing,SS.AbstractGröbnerBasisAlgorithm},\n    S<:Union{Nothing,SS.AbstractAlgebraicSolver},\n}\n    algorithm::A = nothing\n    solver::S = nothing\nend\n\nSolve a border basis using algorithm and solver by first converting it to aBorderBasis{D}`.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.AlgebraicBorderSolver"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.AlgebraicFallbackBorderSolver","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct AlgebraicFallbackBorderSolver{\n    M<:Union{Nothing,SS.AbstractMultiplicationMatricesSolver},\n    S<:AlgebraicBorderSolver,\n}\n    multiplication_matrices_solver::M\n    algebraic_solver::S\nend\n\nSolve with multiplication_matrices_solver and if it fails, falls back to solving the algebraic system formed by the border basis with algebraic_solver.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.AlgebraicFallbackBorderSolver"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.MomentMatrixWeightSolver","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct MomentMatrixWeightSolver\n    rtol::T\n    atol::T\nend\n\nGiven a moment matrix ν and the atom centers, determine the weights by solving a linear system over all the moments of the moment matrix, keeping duplicates (e.g., entries corresponding to the same monomial).\n\nIf the moment values corresponding to the same monomials are known to be equal prefer MomentVectorWeightSolver instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.MomentMatrixWeightSolver"},{"category":"type","location":"atoms/#MultivariateMoments.MomentVectorWeightSolver","page":"Atoms extraction","text":"struct MomentVectorWeightSolver{T}\n    rtol::T\n    atol::T\nend\n\nGiven a moment matrix ν and the atom centers, first convert the moment matrix to a vector of moments, using moment_vector(ν; rtol=rtol, atol=atol) and then determine the weights by solving a linear system over the monomials obtained.\n\nIf the moment values corresponding to the same monomials can have small differences, moment_vector can throw an error if rtol and atol are not small enough. Alternatively to tuning these tolerances MomentVectorWeightSolver can be used instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultivariateMoments.MomentVectorWeightSolver"}]
}
